2026-05-09 16:00:41,845 - INFO - Response 1: Your friend has the stronger case here, though it's genuinely a fun debate.
2026-05-09 16:00:41,845 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:00:41,845 - INFO - **Why a hot dog usually isn't considered a sandwich:**
2026-05-09 16:00:41,845 - INFO - The most common definition of a sandwich is two *separate* pieces of bread with filling between them. A hot dog has one piece of bread that's hinged—it's a single entity folded, not two distinct pieces.
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - **The counterargument (your side):**
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - By a very broad structural definition—bread enclosing a filling—a hot dog technically qualifies. So does a taco, a burrito, or a pita.
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - **The practical answer:**
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - In everyday language, "sandwich" and "hot dog" are distinct categories. No one orders a sandwich and receives a hot dog. They're understood as different things. This matters more than pure structural logic.
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:00:41,846 - INFO - That said, if you want to win the argument philosophically, you could argue that the distinction is ultimately arbitrary—but you'd likely be fighting against common usage rather than winning on the merits.
