2026-05-09 16:02:10,544 - INFO - Response 2: # Neither is categorically right—it depends on the metric.
2026-05-09 16:02:10,544 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:10,544 - INFO - **Private healthcare often has lower costs for *individual procedures*** due to competition and efficiency incentives. A specific surgery might cost less at a private clinic than a government facility.
2026-05-09 16:02:10,544 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:10,544 - INFO - **Government healthcare typically has lower *overall system costs*** as a percentage of GDP. Countries like Germany, Canada, and Australia spend 9-12% of GDP on healthcare, while the US (largely private) spends ~17%. This is because government systems:
2026-05-09 16:02:10,544 - INFO - - Negotiate drug/service prices more aggressively
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - - Reduce administrative overhead
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - - Eliminate profit margins
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - **The catch:** Lower overall costs sometimes mean longer wait times or restricted access to certain treatments.
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - **Most honest answer:** The US private system has high costs but fast access. Most developed countries' mixed/public systems have lower costs but potentially longer waits. Each trades off differently between cost, speed, and comprehensiveness.
2026-05-09 16:02:10,545 - INFO - 
