2026-05-09 16:02:55,036 - INFO - Response 2: This is genuinely complex, and both of you are capturing something real.
2026-05-09 16:02:55,036 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:55,036 - INFO - **Your friend has stronger scientific support.** Addiction involves measurable brain changes—altered reward pathways, reduced impulse control, and physical dependence. People can become addicted despite best intentions, and willpower alone often fails. It meets medical definitions of disease.
2026-05-09 16:02:55,036 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:55,036 - INFO - **Your point isn't entirely wrong though.** Initial drug use *is* a choice. And treatment does require behavioral change and personal agency. But calling it "just a choice" misses why some people get addicted and others don't, and why addiction is so hard to escape through sheer willpower.
2026-05-09 16:02:55,037 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:55,037 - INFO - **The useful framing:** Initial use involves choice, but addiction becomes a condition where choice becomes severely constrained—similar to how diabetes involves behaviors but isn't simply a "choice disease." This distinction matters practically: it shapes whether we treat people punitively or medically.
2026-05-09 16:02:55,037 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:02:55,037 - INFO - The either/or framing ("pure choice" vs. "pure disease") both oversimplifies. Most experts now see addiction as involving biological vulnerability, environmental factors, *and* behavioral elements—where personal responsibility matters but isn't the whole story.
