2026-05-09 16:04:11,888 - INFO - Response 1: Both of you are pointing at something real, so it's not a clean either/or.
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - **The research shows:**
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - - Children *generally* do better with two engaged, stable parents than one—more resources, attention, and support matter
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - - But single parents raise plenty of thriving kids
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - - What matters far more than the number of parents: stability, emotional warmth, financial security, and freedom from high conflict
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - **So the nuance:**
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - - Your point has statistical backing: two parents *on average* correlates with better outcomes
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - - Your friend's point is also true: one parent can absolutely be sufficient, and a single stable parent often beats two conflicted ones
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - - A child's thriving depends much more on what parents *do* than how many there are
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - The strongest version of each position: "Two stable, involved parents is ideal *all else equal*" and "One loving, stable parent is far better than two unhappy or absent ones" are both defensible.
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:11,889 - INFO - Where you might actually disagree: whether the difference is about the *number* of parents or about the stability and resources available. That's probably the more useful argument to have.
