2026-05-09 16:04:35,754 - INFO - Response 1: Your friend has the stronger case here.
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - **Why "always good" is hard to defend:**
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - - Forgiving someone who shows no remorse and will harm others again might enable further damage
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - - Forgiving can sometimes let people avoid accountability for serious wrongs
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - - Pressure to forgive can silence victims and deny the legitimacy of their hurt
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - - Forgiving yourself prematurely for a mistake might prevent necessary change
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - **What's actually true:**
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - Forgiveness is *often* beneficial—it can reduce bitterness, enable healing, and restore relationships. But it's contextual. Whether it's good depends on:
2026-05-09 16:04:35,755 - INFO - - Whether the person has shown genuine change
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - - What risks exist if you let your guard down
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - - Whether it serves your actual wellbeing or just appeases others
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - - The severity and pattern of harm
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - **The nuance your friend is pointing at:** Healthy forgiveness is usually a *choice* you make when it serves you, not an obligation. Sometimes the healthier move is setting boundaries, walking away, or holding someone accountable—at least initially.
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:04:35,756 - INFO - So while forgiveness is often wise, treating it as always good can actually cause harm.
