2026-05-09 16:05:57,628 - INFO - Response 1: You're right, though I'd note your friend isn't entirely wrong either—it's genuinely complex.
2026-05-09 16:05:57,628 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:05:57,628 - INFO - **Why video games qualify as art:**
2026-05-09 16:05:57,628 - INFO - - They involve deliberate creative choices in visuals, sound, narrative, and interaction
2026-05-09 16:05:57,628 - INFO - - They can express ideas, emotions, and commentary (like any traditional medium)
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - - They're recognized by major institutions—museums exhibit them, critics analyze them seriously
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - **Why the disagreement persists:**
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - - Games involve functional/commercial elements that traditional art doesn't always have
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - - Interactivity is a different creative relationship than passive viewing
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - - It's still a young medium, so definitions are still settling
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:05:57,629 - INFO - **The honest take:** Most serious critics and institutions now accept that games *can* be art (especially narrative or artistic games like *Journey* or *Gris*), even if not all games prioritize artistic expression over entertainment or profit.
2026-05-09 16:05:57,630 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:05:57,630 - INFO - Your friend might be reacting to games as primarily entertainment products, which is fair—but that doesn't mean the medium can't be art, any more than Hollywood being commercial means film isn't art.
