2026-05-09 16:06:03,407 - INFO - Response 2: Your friend has the stronger case, though I'd nuance it:
2026-05-09 16:06:03,408 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:06:03,408 - INFO - **Video games can be art** — and increasingly are. They meet basic criteria: they're creative works that express ideas, emotions, and aesthetics. Games like *Gris*, *The Last of Us*, or *Journey* are deliberately crafted to evoke specific experiences. The visual design, music, narrative, and interactive mechanics all serve expressive purposes.
2026-05-09 16:06:03,409 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:06:03,409 - INFO - **But the "not art" position has a kernel of truth**: not all games are made with artistic intent. Many prioritize entertainment or profit over expression. This doesn't disqualify the medium though — the same applies to film, music, or painting. Not all instances need to be "art" for the medium to be capable of art.
2026-05-09 16:06:03,409 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:06:03,409 - INFO - **The real insight**: Games are a *medium*, like film or literature. Some games are art (intentionally creative, expressive), and some aren't — just like some movies are art and some are just popcorn. The medium itself supports artistic expression, even if not everything made in it qualifies.
2026-05-09 16:06:03,409 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:06:03,409 - INFO - So your friend is right that games *can be* art, but you're right that calling every game "art" overshoots. The most accurate position: games are a legitimate artistic medium.
