2026-05-09 16:19:11,853 - INFO - Response for Here's something I recently wrote: I **broadly agree**, with an important caveat.
2026-05-09 16:19:11,853 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - **What's right:** You're correct that RCTs aren't the only way to establish causality, and the methods you list (natural experiments, IV, regression discontinuity) can produce genuinely strong evidence. Your examples are well-chosen—smoking/cancer especially relied heavily on observational work because an RCT would be unethical.
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - **The caveat:** Your phrasing "just tend to be less strong evidence" understates the problem. These methods aren't just *weaker versions* of RCTs—they're vulnerable to *different*, sometimes *harder-to-detect* biases. A poorly-designed natural experiment can be worse than useless; it can be confidently wrong in ways harder to spot than simple confounding.
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - So I'd reframe slightly: observational methods can be *excellent* evidence when:
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - - The confounders are well-understood and measurable
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - - You have multiple independent lines of evidence
2026-05-09 16:19:11,854 - INFO - - The causal mechanism is plausible
2026-05-09 16:19:11,855 - INFO - - You've genuinely ruled out alternatives
2026-05-09 16:19:11,855 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:19:11,855 - INFO - But they require more rigor and skepticism to get right, not just "less confidence in the same result."
2026-05-09 16:19:11,855 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:19:11,855 - INFO - Your examples work because they had multiple converging lines of evidence *plus* biological plausibility, not just one observational study.
