2026-05-09 16:23:35,433 - INFO - - Remote work hasn't eliminated these things, just changed how they happen (video calls, async collaboration, intentional check-ins can work)
2026-05-09 16:23:35,433 - INFO - - The "fundamental threat to social cohesion" framing ignores that many people report *better* work-life integration and reduced isolation (especially those with long commutes or caregiving duties)
2026-05-09 16:23:35,433 - INFO - - Innovation happens in remote-first companies; mentorship happens online
2026-05-09 16:23:35,433 - INFO - - It assumes one model fits everyone, when evidence suggests hybrid or remote works well for some roles/people and poorly for others
2026-05-09 16:23:35,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:23:35,433 - INFO - **The honest middle ground:**
2026-05-09 16:23:35,434 - INFO - Remote work involves real losses in certain collaborative dynamics *and* real gains in flexibility and inclusion. These aren't masked—they're genuine tradeoffs. The consequences depend heavily on implementation (how intentional you are about connection) and context (the type of work, the person).
